<title>PHILIPPINES: Manila's long-term PHIBOR rates decline.</title>
<headline>Manila's long-term PHIBOR rates decline.</headline>
<dateline>MANILA 1997-01-15</dateline>
<text>
<p>Philippine interbank offered rates (PHIBOR) were mostly a tad lower especially at the long end of the curve, traders said.</p>
<p>&quot;Some traders were expecting T-bill rates to go down further due to the excess liquidity in the market,&quot; one trader said.</p>
<p>The PHIBOR normally track their corresponding spot rates.</p>
<p>Only the one-week and one-month PHIBOR were steady, as the Central Bank did not change its benchmark rates in the market.</p>
<p>PHIBOR are the average swap rates quoted by local and foreign banks operating in the country.  </p>
<p>Average rates quoted by 20 banks were:</p>
<p>			 JAN 15		  JAN 14</p>
<p>    1 W		10  1/4		 10  1/4</p>
<p>    2 W		10  3/16		10  1/4</p>
<p>    1 M		10  1/8		 10  1/8</p>
<p>    2 M		10  3/16		10  1/4</p>
<p>    3 M		10  3/16		10  1/4</p>
<p>    6 M		10  7/16		10  1/2</p>
<p>    12M		10 11/16		10  3/4</p>
<p>    VAL		16-Jan		  15-Jan</p>
